= Constraints and stack

Most design decisions in Code Share follow from a small number of constraints. Each constraint
excludes an otherwise obvious solution, and they are therefore stated here before the design.

== A free Apple developer account

The app is not distributed through the App Store, so the paid programme (\$99 per year) was not
purchased. A free _Personal Team_ (the same team used by the holy-shit iOS app) can sign and
sideload builds onto the owner's own phones, subject to the following limits:

#dtable(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, 1.2fr),
  header: ("Missing", "Would have been used for", "What Code Share does instead"),
  [Push (APNs)], [Notifying the other members' phones that a code was added or used], [Sync on foreground, 2 s after an edit, on pull-to-refresh],
  [Sign in with Apple], [Accounts], [Passkeys on a server-hosted page in `ASWebAuthenticationSession` (the holy-shit approach)],
  [Associated Domains], [Native passkey APIs bound to the domain], [Same page-based flow, custom URL scheme callback],
  [iCloud / CloudKit], [Syncing without a backend], [Own Go backend],
  [TestFlight], [Distributing to other members' phones], [Sideload; builds expire after *7 days* and must be reinstalled],
  [App Groups (unverified)], [Database shared with widget and share extension], [Entitlement added; whether a free team can provision it remains to be checked in Xcode],
)

The seven-day expiry is more consequential than it appears. Any build on a phone is at most one
week old, so there is no long tail of old app versions requiring compatibility. A short tail does
exist, however, and @sec-wipe describes a case in which an old build on one phone constrained a
server change.

== Platform floor and appearance

The phones run iOS 18 and iOS 26, but the code is kept portable to *iOS 16*, the minimum version
of VisionKit's `DataScannerViewController`. This excludes several recent APIs:

- `ObservableObject` and `@Published` are used instead of the `@Observable` macro (iOS 17).
- SwiftData (iOS 17) is not available; together with the reasons given in @sec-grdb, this led
  to SQLite via GRDB.
- The single-argument `onChange(of:)` is used, because the two-argument form requires iOS 17.

*No Liquid Glass.* `ios/Config/Info.plist` sets `UIDesignRequiresCompatibility`, so on iOS 26
the app retains the iOS 18 appearance, as holy-shit-ios does. This file is merged with the
generated plist, which also declares the `codeshare://` URL scheme.

== A shared server

The backend runs on a single FreeBSD machine that already serves other production applications
(the author's other projects). The machine is managed by Ansible in a separate
repository, `shared-infrastructure`, which also contains Terraform/terragrunt units for the AWS
resources of each project (CloudWatch logs and error e-mails, S3 backups). Because the project is
one tenant on a shared production host, operations follow a conservative procedure: dry runs
first, changes confined to the project's own resources, reload rather than restart, and
verification of the neighbouring services afterwards (Chapter 8).

The shared host also constrained the backend: no Docker, a single static binary, and a PostgreSQL
instance shared with the other applications (so unauthenticated endpoints must not be able to
exhaust its disk).

